H("Abstract")
P("Statutes are amended continually, and a legal question-answering system must follow them. When a provision changes, drafters list the consequential amendments that other provisions need. A model that holds statutory text in its weights faces a similar problem: an update that states some amended provisions may leave related ones at their old values. We ask how common update procedures behave, using synthetic fact banks on models of 70 million to 7 billion parameters and 27 real amendments to the Employment Rights Act 1996 and the Companies Act 2006, set against 66 unchanged provisions. We compare fine-tuning with several retention constraints, MEMIT, ROME, in-context editing and retrieval over a document store. Retaining everything the update does not mention helps only when updates can be matched to stored provisions accurately, with a break-even near a matching accuracy of 0.7 to 0.8. Provisions whose values follow arithmetically from an amended value did not follow in any procedure unless they were listed, and listing them restored them. In real statutes, provisions that share wording followed an amendment stated in only a few of them, and holding such provisions at their old value was harmful. Unconstrained fine-tuning also shifted unrelated provisions towards a nearby value; a scoped constraint prevented this. Retrieval over a store refreshed only for the known amendments left unlisted and dependent provisions at their old values, as the weight updates did, whereas a fully refreshed store did not. The evidence covers 27 provisions, two Acts and one model family.", align=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.JUSTIFY)
P("Keywords: Statutory amendment; Consequential amendment; Knowledge editing; Language models; Legal information systems; Continual learning", italic=True)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1 Introduction
H("1 Introduction")
P("A language model that answers questions about the law keeps what it knows in its weights. When a rule changes, someone has to change the model, or the system will go on stating the old rule with the same confidence as before. The consequences are not hypothetical. In Moffatt v. Air Canada (2024 BCCRT 149), a Canadian tribunal held a company to the statement its chatbot had made about a policy, and rejected the argument that the chatbot was a separate entity. Organisations that put models in front of the public will increasingly be asked to show that what the model says matches the rules currently in force.")
P("Keeping a model current is more than replacing one answer. Legislative drafters have long dealt with the same difficulty on paper. When a provision is amended, other provisions may refer to it, repeat its wording or depend on a value it fixes, and the amending instrument usually has to deal with those too. Drafting guidance describes these as consequential amendments, that is, additional amendments required as a result of the main changes, and it advises drafters to search systematically for every cross-reference to the provision being changed (Office of Parliamentary Counsel 2025a). A separate set of rules, transitional and saving provisions, determines which version applies to cases that arose before the change, and so preserves the old law for those cases (Office of Parliamentary Counsel 2025b); it does not shield provisions that the amendment leaves untouched. A model update that ignores the first of these practices can leave the model in a state that no legislature intended: the amended provision is new, its dependents are old, and nothing in the system records the difference.")
P("The model-editing literature has measured this problem under another name. Editing one fact can imply changes to related facts, and editors that change the target fact often fail to change what follows from it (Cohen et al. 2024; Zhong et al. 2023). That work uses encyclopaedic facts, mostly edited one at a time. A body of statute differs in ways that matter for the question. Amendments arrive in groups. The same sentence is often repeated across many provisions, so that an amendment to a few of them changes the evidence for the rest. Some provisions contain values that are computed from others. And the conservative instinct of editing research, to leave everything else alone, collides with the fact that some of the ‘everything else’ is exactly what the amendment was meant to change.")
P("This paper asks how far familiar update procedures handle the situation that consequential amendments address. We treat an amendment as a change to a share of a bank of provisions, apply several update procedures to a model that has learned the earlier text, and measure what happens to the amended provisions, to the provisions that were not amended, and to provisions that depend on the amended ones. Retrieval over a current text is the obvious alternative to updating weights, and we include it as a baseline and discuss when weights need updating at all (Sections 6.6 and 7.2). We make four contributions.")
BL(["We formalise the update of a model after an amendment in terms borrowed from drafting practice, and separate three outcomes that are often run together: currency (amended provisions take the new value), continuity (untouched provisions keep theirs) and ripple (dependent provisions follow).",
    "In synthetic fact banks on models from 70 million to 7 billion parameters, we show that the best strength of a retention constraint depends on the share of provisions that were superseded, and that retaining everything the update does not mention reaches the attainable ceiling only to the extent that updates can be matched to stored provisions. An accounting identity under stated assumptions describes the outcome in the settings we tested.",
    "We confirm, in statutory text, a dependence already reported for arithmetic and multi-hop consequences of edits (Zhong et al. 2023; Cohen et al. 2024): provisions whose values follow arithmetically from an amended value did not follow on their own in any procedure or at any model size we tested. We add a measurement of what listing the dependents does, which is a design implication more than a discovery: listed dependents were restored completely.",
    "We assemble 27 real amendments to two United Kingdom Acts from the legislation.gov.uk record, together with 66 unchanged provisions as controls, and evaluate collateral damage against a plausible alternative value and not only against a random number. Provisions that share wording followed an amendment stated in only a few of them, and the retention strategy that works best on synthetic data harmed them. We compare these results with MEMIT, ROME, in-context editing and retrieval, and we show that the weak in-context result on a fine-tuned model is a consequence of the fine-tuning."])
P("The evidence is small and we say so throughout. It consists of one model family, 27 amended provisions from two Acts and a forced-choice evaluation. Section 8 sets out what it does and does not support.")
P("The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 formalises the problem and Section 4 describes the update procedures. Section 5 describes the data and experiments, Section 6 reports the results, and Section 7 discusses their implications for legal information systems. Section 8 sets out limitations and Section 9 concludes.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2 Related work
H("2 Background and related work")
H("2.1 Consequential, transitional and saving provisions", 2)
P("Drafting offices distinguish the change a bill is meant to make from the changes needed to keep the statute book coherent once it is made. Australian drafting guidance, for example, defines a consequential amendment as an additional amendment required as a result of the main changes in a legislative project, and notes that it can be purely technical, such as correcting a cross-reference, or substantive, such as reworking a concept (Office of Parliamentary Counsel 2025a). The practical advice is to search the legislation for every form of reference to the provision being amended, including references to the wider part or division, and to consider structural effects such as an orphaned heading. This is the practice to which our listing procedure corresponds: deciding which other provisions must change along with the amended one.")
P("Transitional and saving provisions do something different, and the difference matters for the comparison. Because laws generally operate prospectively, an amending instrument often says that the old text continues to apply to cases, rights or proceedings that arose before commencement (Office of Parliamentary Counsel 2025b). A saving provision therefore preserves the old law for a class of past cases. It does not protect provisions that the amendment leaves untouched, because those need no protection. Both the old and the new text remain valid, each for its own cases. The retention constraints we use in training have no counterpart in this practice. They are a technical device against forgetting, and we do not claim that they implement a saving clause. Drafting practice bears on our experiments only through consequential amendments; the issue of validity at a point in time is taken up in Section 7.5.")
P("Computational work on legislation has long treated versions and amendments as structured data. Standards for legal documents represent a provision as a sequence of versions with dates (Palmirani and Vitali 2011), and temporal models for normative systems distinguish, among other things, the time at which a text is in force from the time at which a norm is applied and from the time at which it is efficacious (Palmirani and Brighi 2006). That structure makes it possible to retrieve the text in force immediately before an amendment, as we do in Section 5. In AI and Law, norm change has been analysed as revision, derogation and annulment of rules in a defeasible logic, so that a modification of one norm can change the status of others (Governatori et al. 2005), and in the logic of theory change, revising a belief set minimally while preserving its consistency is a central problem (Alchourrón et al. 1985). A consequential amendment can be read as an instance of the same problem in a setting where the constraints are the dependencies between provisions. We use this reading as motivation only and make no formal claim. Our setting is narrower than these models: it has a single current version and no dates on the queries.")
H("2.2 Knowledge editing and ripple effects", 2)
P("Methods for changing a single fact in a language model without retraining include locating and rewriting the relevant weights (Meng et al. 2022; Meng et al. 2023), training an auxiliary editor (De Cao et al. 2021; Mitchell et al. 2022) and supplying the new fact in the prompt (Zheng et al. 2023); see Yao et al. (2023) for a survey. Evaluations that go beyond the edited fact find that related facts frequently fail to change. Cohen et al. (2024) build a benchmark of about 5,000 edits with test queries for their logical consequences and report that existing editors often change the target fact without keeping related knowledge consistent, and that a simple in-context baseline did best. Zhong et al. (2023) show that edited models answer paraphrases of the edited fact but fail on multi-hop questions that depend on it. Our experiments differ in three respects: the facts are provisions of statutes, the amendments come in groups with shared wording, and we compare editors with a fine-tuning procedure whose retention constraint can be varied.")
H("2.3 Continual learning and temporal misalignment", 2)
P("Fine-tuning a model on new text can erase earlier competence (French 1999; Kirkpatrick et al. 2017), and standard remedies include replaying earlier data (Rolnick et al. 2019) and constraining the updated model to stay close to the earlier one. Language models also fall out of date as the world changes, which has been studied as temporal generalisation (Lazaridou et al. 2021; Dhingra et al. 2022). The constraint to stay close to an earlier model is exactly what an amendment must not apply to the amended provision, a tension that has received less attention than forgetting itself and that we examine directly.")
H("2.4 Retrieval and legal question answering", 2)
P("Retrieval-augmented generation supplies passages from an external store to the model at question time (Lewis et al. 2020), and it is the usual answer to out-of-date parametric knowledge. Legal question answering has been a main application, and evaluations of language models and of commercial legal research tools document substantial rates of hallucination, including errors about the current status of authorities (Dahl et al. 2024; Magesh et al. 2025). Retrieval does not remove the question we study. It moves it from the weights to the store: a store that contains the old text of a dependent provision returns the old text, and an index refreshed only for the amendments someone has listed has the same gap as a model updated only on them. We test this in Section 6.6.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3 Problem formulation
H("3 Problem formulation")
P("A fact is a pair (p, v) in which p is a prompt that identifies a provision, for example the statutory text up to the point where a value is stated, and v is the value that completes it. A launch model has learned a bank of N facts with values v₀. An amendment changes the authorised value of a subset of the bank. We write ρ for the share of facts in the bank whose value is superseded. A fact is a dependent of another if its authorised value is determined by the value of the other. In the synthetic banks this is a fixed mapping between two sets of values, and in the real data it is the relation between a net threshold and the corresponding gross threshold.")
P("An update procedure receives an update stream, a set of statements of amended provisions with their new values, and may in addition use a retention constraint that keeps selected facts at their launch behaviour. We say that a fact is listed if its amended statement appears in the stream. The share of listed facts among those that are amended is written k. For some experiments we also vary the coverage c, the share of superseded facts that the stream mentions at all, and the matching accuracy m, the probability that a fact mentioned in the stream is recognised as the stored fact it amends, so that its retention can be released.")
P("After the update we measure the following outcomes, each as a share of the relevant facts.")
BL(["Currency: amended provisions for which the model prefers the new value to the old one.",
    "Continuity: provisions that were not amended and for which the model still prefers the launch value. One minus continuity is the collateral damage.",
    "Ripple: dependent provisions of amended provisions that are answered with the new derived value, reported separately for dependents that were and were not listed.",
    "Acquisition: new facts that appear in the stream and are learned."])
P("Preference is judged by forced choice: we compare the log-probability that the model assigns to the authorised value with that of a competitor, which is the old value for an amended provision. This avoids the problem that many of the real values are several tokens long and share their first token (for example ‘£10.2 million’ and ‘£15 million’). For an unamended control provision we use two competitors, a random number of the same kind, which is an easy test, and a different period of the kind that occurs in the Acts (for example ‘six’ for ‘three’), which is a hard one. We also report the mean change in the log-probability margin of the true control value, which is not capped at one. Forced choice measures a preference between two answers and does not show that the model would produce the correct answer unprompted. Throughout, the authorised value is the value in force today; queries about the law at an earlier date are not modelled (Section 7.5).")
P("A simple identity helps to interpret the results. Suppose that a procedure retains every fact the update does not mention and releases only those facts that are matched to a listed amendment. Suppose further that unamended facts are retained perfectly, that matched and released facts take the new value, and that nothing else changes. Then currency is (1 − ρ) + ρcm by definition, where the first term is the share of facts that are not amended and the second the share of amended facts that the update both mentions and releases. The identity is an accounting statement, not a prediction about model behaviour. What the experiments test is whether its assumptions hold in practice. They did at the settings we ran (Section 6.2), and we do not claim more.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4 Update procedures
H("4 Update procedures and their drafting counterparts")
P("We compare the procedures in Table 1. The first five rows are variants of one fine-tuning procedure that differ only in the retention constraint or in what the update stream contains. The remaining rows are established editors and a retrieval baseline applied to the same launch model. Only the listing row has a counterpart in drafting practice; the retention rows are technical devices.")
Tab("Update procedures and the drafting practice each is closest to", [
    ["Procedure", "What it does", "Closest drafting practice"],
    ["Amend only", "Fine-tune on the statements of listed amendments; no constraint", "None (the amending text replaces the old text)"],
    ["Retain all", "Add a KL penalty to the launch model on every launch fact (weight λ)", "None; a technical device against forgetting"],
    ["Stream-scoped retention", "KL penalty on launch facts that the update does not mention", "None; a technical device"],
    ["Dependency-scoped retention", "As stream-scoped, but facts that belong to the amendment (siblings, dependents) are also released", "None; a technical device that needs to know which provisions the amendment touches"],
    ["Consequential listing", "Dependents are also stated in the update stream (completeness k_dep)", "Consequential amendments listed in the amending instrument"],
    ["MEMIT, ROME", "Rank-one weight edits located in the model (EasyEdit implementation)", "None; external baselines"],
    ["In-context editing (IKE)", "Amended statements placed in the prompt; weights unchanged", "None; external baseline"],
    ["Retrieval (RAG)", "Top three statements retrieved from a store by TF-IDF are placed in the prompt; the store is refreshed for listed amendments only (incremental) or for all (full)", "A consolidated, up-to-date text of the Act"]],
    note="KL, Kullback–Leibler divergence on the distribution over the answer tokens.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5 Experimental design
H("5 Data and experimental design")
H("5.1 Synthetic fact banks", 2)
P("To control the structure of an amendment we first use synthetic banks. In the first, a provision is identified by a rule code and its value is a two-digit number or a category word, with 200 launch facts and 50 further facts that appear only in the update. In the second, the facts are provisions of fictional statutes (‘Under Article 14 of the Housing Subsidy Act, an application must be filed within’, completed by a spelled-out number), each stated in three paraphrases, so that an amendment given in one wording has to be recognised in the others. In the third, each base provision is paired with a dependent whose text refers to it and whose value is a fixed function of the base value (a filing deadline determines a fee tier). An amendment replaces a share ρ of base values and the authorised value of each dependent changes accordingly. In a fourth variant the launch data also contain explicit statements of the mapping.")
H("5.2 Real amendments", 2)
P("The real data come from legislation.gov.uk, which publishes the text of an Act as it stood on a given date together with editorial notes on each amendment. We scanned sections 1 to 235 of the Employment Rights Act 1996 and sections 380 to 480 of the Companies Act 2006, and also sections 1 to 45 of the Housing Act 1988 and 1 to 130 of the Insolvency Act 1986, which yielded only renumbering changes. We compared the text in force immediately before each amendment came into force with the latest revised text and kept changes of periods and amounts. Each change was matched to the site’s own amendment note, which names the amending instrument and the date of commencement. The result is 27 amended provisions in three groups (Table 2), listed in Appendix A.")
Tab("Real amendments used in the experiments", [
    ["Group", "Provisions", "Change", "In force", "Instrument"],
    ["A. Time limits", "Employment Rights Act 1996, ss. 11, 23, 34, 48, 51, 54, 57, 60, 63, 70, 111, 188 (12)", "period for presenting a complaint: three to six months", "1 Oct 2026", "Employment Rights Act 2025 (c. 36), s. 159(3), Sch. 12; SI 2026/954"],
    ["B. Amounts", "Employment Rights Act 1996, ss. 31, 186, 227 (3)", "£39 to £41 (s. 31); £719 to £751 (ss. 186, 227)", "6 Apr 2026", "Employment Rights (Increase of Limits) Order 2026, SI 2026/310"],
    ["C. Thresholds", "Companies Act 2006, ss. 382, 383, 465, 466 (12 values)", "size thresholds, e.g. turnover £10.2m to £15m; gross values about 1.2 times net", "6 Apr 2025", "SI 2024/1303"]],
    note="Values were taken from the text in force the day before commencement. SI, statutory instrument.")
P("Two features of this material matter for the questions we ask. The twelve time limits in group A were changed by a single amending schedule and are worded almost identically, so they are siblings in the sense of Section 3. In group C each ‘gross’ threshold in sections 383 and 466 is about 1.2 times the corresponding ‘net’ threshold, both before the amendment (10.2 and 12.2, 5.1 and 6.1, 36 and 43.2, 18 and 21.6) and after it (15 and 18, 7.5 and 9, 54 and 64, 27 and 32, the new gross values being rounded). The gross values are therefore dependents whose values follow arithmetically from the net ones. This is an observation about the text and not a rule stated in the Act.")
P("As controls we took 90 candidate phrases from the same Acts that state a period (for example a number of days or weeks) and whose wording and value are identical in the enacted and the current text. We read all 90 and removed 24: six whose prompt began inside a quotation of substituted words, seven with an ambiguous opening (for example ‘the amount of [one] week’), seven near-duplicates of another control and four with an unclear antecedent. This left 66 controls, which with the 27 amended provisions make a bank of 93 facts. The curation was done by one person after the first runs, using only the text of the prompts and not model outputs, and the removed items are listed in the deposit. Each fact is presented as the Act, section number and heading followed by the statutory words up to the value. Some of the remaining passages may still begin mid-sentence, because they are fragments of a longer provision.")
H("5.3 Models, training and evaluation", 2)
P("All procedures start from a launch model obtained by fine-tuning a pretrained model with LoRA (Hu et al. 2022; rank 16, learning rate 2 × 10⁻⁴) on the pre-amendment value of every fact until the token accuracy on the training facts reached 0.995, which happened in every run. The main model is Qwen2.5-7B (Yang et al. 2024); smaller models (Qwen2.5-1.5B and -3B, and a 70-million-parameter model fine-tuned in full) were used for the synthetic experiments. The update is a fresh LoRA adapter trained for 20 to 25 epochs at a learning rate of 10⁻⁴. Retention is implemented as a KL penalty, computed over the distribution of the answer tokens, between the updated model and the launch model on a set of anchor facts, with weight λ = 10 unless stated otherwise (1 and 10 for retention of all facts). MEMIT and ROME were applied to the same launch model through the EasyEdit library (Wang et al. 2024) with the library’s configuration for this model family. The edited subject is a span that occurs in the prompt: the Act and section reference for time limits and amounts, and the last four words of the context for table cells. Covariance statistics were estimated from 10,000 wikitext-103 samples and not from the 100,000 Wikipedia samples of the original setting, and hyperparameters were not tuned. In-context editing and retrieval were run on the pretrained model before launch fine-tuning, on a model fine-tuned for three and ten epochs, and on the launch model (Section 6.6). Retrieval used TF-IDF over unigrams and bigrams of the provision reference and the last words of each statement, with three statements per question.")
P(f"A random draw determines which amended provisions are listed. For each setting it was the same across procedures, so that procedures are compared on identical updates. Seeds change that draw, the distractor numbers, the order of training examples and the initialisation of the adapters. We report means and the range over seeds (three for the synthetic and retrieval experiments, and {SEEDW} for the real-amendment fine-tuning runs) and do not compute significance tests, because so few seeds do not allow them. Code, fact banks and results are available in the repository named in the Declarations.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6 Results
H("6 Results")
H("6.1 The strength of retention and the share of superseded facts", 2)
P("In the first synthetic bank, the best strength of the retention penalty depends on how much of the bank was superseded (Fig. 1; Table 3). With no penalty, fine-tuning on the amended facts forgets many of the untouched ones, so currency is low when few facts were amended. A moderate penalty (λ = 1) protects the untouched facts and gives the best currency up to ρ = 0.5. When most of the bank was superseded, however, the same penalty holds back facts that should change, and the unconstrained update is best from ρ = 0.7. A strong penalty (λ = 10) retains almost all launch behaviour, so its currency is close to 1 − ρ at every value of ρ. The pattern is the same in the 70-million-parameter model with three seeds and in the 1.5-billion-parameter model with one seed.")
def e1(rows, kind):
    out = {}
    for r in rows:
        if kind == "70m" and r["q"] is not None: continue
        if kind == "15b" and r["q"] is not None: continue
        out.setdefault((r["rho"], r["lam"]), []).append(r["currency"])
    return out
a70 = e1(res70, "70m"); a15 = e1(q15, "15b")
rows = [["ρ", "1.5B, λ = 0", "λ = 1", "λ = 10", "70M, λ = 0", "λ = 1", "λ = 10"]]
for rho in (0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9):
    rows.append([rho] + [ms(a15[(rho, l)]) if (rho, l) in a15 else "n/a" for l in (0, 1, 10)] + [ms(a70[(rho, l)]) if (rho, l) in a70 else "n/a" for l in (0, 1, 10)])
Tab("Currency by share of superseded facts (ρ) and strength of retention (λ), independent facts", rows,
    note="1.5B: Qwen2.5-1.5B, one seed. 70M: 70-million-parameter model, mean over two or three seeds depending on the cell.")
