# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7 Discussion
H("7 Discussion")
H("7.1 Two kinds of propagation", 2)
P("The results separate two ways in which an amendment can reach a provision that was not amended in the update. Provisions that share wording with an amended provision followed it without being listed, to a degree that depended little on how many were listed. Provisions whose values are computed from an amended value did not follow at all. A plausible reading is that the first kind of propagation is a by-product of learning a repeated pattern, so that changing the value in a few sentences changes it in the others, whereas the second requires the model to carry out a calculation or a lookup that fine-tuning on the amended statements does not teach. We did not test this explanation. The second finding is not new in kind, since failures on multi-hop and arithmetic consequences of an edit are the main result of Zhong et al. (2023) and Cohen et al. (2024); what the statutory material adds is that the first kind of propagation, which those benchmarks do not have, also occurs and is uncontrolled, since it reaches provisions that should not have followed as well.")
H("7.2 Why update weights?", 2)
P("A reviewer of work like this will ask why one should update weights at all, when a system can retrieve the current text of the law. There are good reasons to prefer retrieval, and our results support them in one respect: over a store that holds the current text of every provision, retrieval gave the highest currency and restored the dependents (Section 6.6). Maintaining a consolidated text is also what legislation services already do, and it keeps a record of what the system was told. Where a current, complete store exists and the system can be built around it, retrieval is the better choice and a weight update adds risk.")
P("Weight updates remain relevant in three situations. The first is a model that has been fine-tuned for a domain and must be kept current without being retrained from scratch, a situation which our launch model imitates. The second is any pipeline in which the model is expected to answer without access to the store, for example in on-device use, in a long chain of reasoning where retrieval is called once, or when retrieval returns nothing. The third is the fact that retrieval does not remove the dependency problem. It moves it into the store. An index refreshed only for the amendments that someone listed (rag_incremental) left the unlisted time limits and the dependent thresholds at their old values, exactly as fine-tuning on the listed amendments did, while the pretrained model with all listed statements in the prompt could follow only the provisions that repeated the listed wording. Whatever the architecture, something has to determine which other provisions are affected by an amendment, and this is the task that a list of consequential amendments carries out in drafting. This is also where the legal setting matters: studies of retrieval-based legal research tools still find substantial rates of hallucination (Magesh et al. 2025), so the currency of the store is a property that has to be tested, not assumed.")
H("7.3 Implications for update practice", 2)
P("Three recommendations follow, each of which should be treated as a hypothesis to test on larger and more varied material. First, an update pipeline should ingest the consequential amendments that the amending instrument already contains, and where dependencies are computed rather than copied it should generate the dependent statements explicitly. This holds for weight updates and for retrieval stores alike. Second, blanket retention should be avoided. A penalty that holds everything not mentioned in the update at its old value was harmful when amended provisions share wording with listed ones, and any retention should be scoped to exclude the provisions that belong to the amendment. Scoped retention did have one clear benefit in the real data: it prevented unrelated period provisions from drifting towards the amended value, which an easy control test did not detect. Third, evaluation after an update should probe three groups separately: the amended provisions, siblings and dependents that were not listed, and a set of unrelated controls, with controls tested against a plausible alternative and not a random one. A single aggregate accuracy would have hidden each of the effects reported here.")
H("7.4 Relation to drafting practice", 2)
P("The comparison with drafting practice should not be pressed far. Drafters work with a known set of provisions and an authoritative cross-reference structure, and the consequential amendments they write are judged by lawyers. A model has no such structure, and what we call a list is something the update procedure has to be given. Only the listing procedure corresponds to a drafting practice, namely the consequential amendment. Retention constraints are a device against forgetting in neural networks and have no counterpart in saving or transitional provisions, which preserve the old law for earlier cases and say nothing about unamended text. What the experiments do support is a narrower claim: the discipline of identifying which other provisions an amendment touches does not come for free from standard update procedures, and for dependencies that are computed it has to be supplied from outside the model.")
H("7.5 Validity at a point in time", 2)
P("Our evaluation treats the value in force today as the only correct answer and the earlier value as an error. That is the right reading for a question about the current law, but it is not how law works. Legal systems keep the earlier text valid for events that occurred while it was in force, and saving provisions often extend it to proceedings begun later. A system that answers questions of the form ‘what was the time limit for a complaint arising in March 2026?’ needs the dated versions and the transitional rules, which models of legal time represent explicitly (Palmirani and Brighi 2006; Governatori et al. 2005). Updating weights to overwrite the old value erases the old law. We did not measure how well the updated models still answer questions about the earlier period, and the forced choice we use would count the old value as wrong. For this reason we consider our results relevant to questions about the current law only, and we think that versioned retrieval, and not an overwriting weight update, is the more natural design for time-sensitive legal questions. Extending the benchmark with date-qualified queries is the most important next step.")

H("8 Limitations")
P(f"The study has clear limits. The real amendments are 27 provisions from two United Kingdom Acts, found by scanning four Acts, and they are not a sample of legislation. Only one model family was used and almost all of the real-data results come from one model size with LoRA fine-tuning, so the results may not hold for other families, for full fine-tuning or for production retrieval systems; our retrieval is a simple TF-IDF index over short statements and not a legal retrieval system. The real-amendment fine-tuning results rest on {SEEDW}. The old values were learned by fine-tuning at launch and were not part of what the model knew from pretraining, which makes the experiments controlled but unlike a deployed system, and as Section 6.6 shows, it changes how the model uses context. Evaluation is by forced choice between two values and does not show what a model would generate. The synthetic dependency is a one-step mapping, and the real dependency is one arithmetic relation. The identity for currency was written down after the first experiments and holds only when its assumptions do. The established editors were not tuned, their statistics were estimated from a smaller sample than in their original setting, and our fine-tuning procedure had the advantage of the same training route as the launch model. The 66 controls were selected by one person from 90 candidates, and the text of the extracted passages was checked against the amendment notes but not proofread in full. Extent and territorial variants of the Acts and transitional rules were not examined, validity at a point in time is not modelled, and nothing in the paper is legal advice.")

H("9 Conclusion")
P("When a statute is amended, drafting practice supplies a rule for what the amendment does to other provisions: the drafter lists the consequential amendments. We compared update procedures for language models on synthetic fact banks and on 27 real amendments, and we asked what the same problem looks like for a model that holds statutory text. Retaining what the update does not mention is useful only as far as updates can be matched to stored provisions, and it can be harmful when unmentioned provisions share wording with the amended ones. Dependent provisions whose values are computed from an amended value do not follow unless they are listed, whether the knowledge sits in the weights or in an incrementally refreshed retrieval store; a fully refreshed store avoids the problem, and is the better design where one can be maintained. Overwriting weights also discards the earlier law, which matters for date-qualified questions. The evidence is small, and its main use is to indicate what a larger study should measure: amended provisions, unlisted siblings and dependents, and controls tested against a hard alternative, for each update procedure, with queries that carry dates.")

